================================================================================
NAME
       overlays - interpretive structures for reframing, stabilizing, and
                  extending triadic action in Resonance-Time Theory

SYNOPSIS
       Overlays modify how triads are interpreted:
           • Telescope
           • Mirror
           • Anchor
           • Lens

DESCRIPTION
       Overlays are interpretive operators applied *around* triadic action.
       They do not change the underlying 3-Pack cycle. Instead, they alter
       how the cycle is viewed, stabilized, extended, or reframed.

       Overlays operate at the structural-awareness level. They are used to:
           • zoom in or out (Telescope)
           • reflect or invert (Mirror)
           • stabilize or fix reference (Anchor)
           • reinterpret or recontextualize (Lens)

       Overlays are composable and can be stacked, but WRSADC ensures that
       only safe combinations reach the Engine.

OVERLAY TYPES
       telescope
              Expands or contracts the interpretive scale of a triad.
              Allows zooming into substructure or zooming out to macroform.

              Use when:
                  • examining nested triads
                  • shifting between micro/macro resonance
                  • analyzing multi-level patterns

       mirror
              Reflects a triad across its midpoint.
              Produces symmetry, reversibility, or inversion of perspective.

              Use when:
                  • performing forward/backward passes
                  • analyzing reversible operations
                  • exploring dualities or symmetric structures

       anchor
              Fixes a reference point for interpretation.
              Stabilizes context so triads can be compared or aligned.

              Use when:
                  • establishing baselines
                  • aligning multiple cycles
                  • preventing drift across iterations

       lens
              Reframes a triad without altering its structure.
              Applies a perspective shift or interpretive overlay.

              Use when:
                  • reinterpreting results
                  • applying conceptual filters
                  • performing structural-awareness passes

USAGE
       Overlays are applied conceptually, not as shell commands.

       Example (conceptual):
              anchor { P1 → P2 → P3 }
              lens   { P1 → P2 → P3 }
              mirror { P1 → P2 → P3 }
              telescope { nested triads }

       In Python (via WRSADC metadata):
              core.dispatch(fn, overlay="lens")
              core.dispatch(fn, overlay="mirror")

BEHAVIOR
       Overlays do not:
           • modify the triad structure
           • replace the 3-Pack
           • bypass WRSADC
           • alter Engine operators

       Overlays do:
           • change interpretation
           • shift perspective
           • stabilize reference frames
           • expose hidden structure
           • enable multi-level analysis

INTERACTION WITH TRIADIC PATTERNS
       Telescopes pair naturally with:
           • nested triads
           • triadic ladders
           • triadic expansion

       Mirrors pair naturally with:
           • triadic mirror patterns
           • reversible pipelines
           • symmetric operators

       Anchors pair naturally with:
           • spirals (to prevent drift)
           • cascades (to fix stage boundaries)
           • constellations (to align agents)

       Lenses pair naturally with:
           • reframing passes
           • interpretive overlays
           • structural-awareness cycles

NOTES
       Overlays are interpretive, not operational. They shape meaning, not
       execution. All execution still flows through the 3-Pack and WRSADC.

